#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Patch the Claude Code VS Code extension so every monospace surface in its
chat follows the user's editor.fontFamily setting, and every such surface
except the inline code in assistant replies and the monospace text after the
tool name in tool call headers (and elsewhere with its CSS class) follows
editor.fontSize too, so its model picker offers a fixed lineup of models, so
its input footer shows how many tokens are in context, so a prompt stands
out, is shown in full, and scrolls with the rest of the chat instead of
staying pinned to its top, so its chat-font text is set in Inter Display
(where that is installed), so the text of assistant replies is restyled for
easier reading, so its markdown tables look less cramped, so its scrollbars
look like VS Code's own, so a Bash (or PowerShell) tool call's command wraps
instead of being cut off at the edge, so a tool call's clipped plain-text
input or output says how many lines it hides instead of fading out, so
copying text from the chat gives markdown, so a click on a commit ID in the
chat shows that commit, so a chat's editor tab shows a spinning logo while
its session runs (background tasks included), so its input footer counts the
running background tasks that are not agents, so the chat keeps a spinner
after a turn while background tasks run, naming them, and, on NixOS, so its
native binary can run at all.

VS Code injects the real editor font into every webview as the
--vscode-editor-font-family / --vscode-editor-font-size (px) CSS variables,
but the extension overwrites both variables from the chat.editor.* settings
(defaults: "monospace" / 12px, i.e. Consolas at 12px on Windows), and several
surfaces hardcode their own sizes besides. This script patches:

  extension.js       - source that override (and its change listener) from
                       editor.* instead of chat.editor.*, making the two
                       variables trustworthy everywhere in the webview;
                       launch the CLI with MODEL_LINEUP as its model picker
                       and DEFAULT_MODEL as its default model, and put the
                       model rows it answers with in lineup order, name-only,
                       re-adding Mythos when the CLI hides it; give a chat
                       tab the RUNNING_ICON while its session is running;
                       have the webview's request to open an address show
                       the commit instead, for a commit link's address
                       (with commit-links.js, from this script's
                       directory)
  resources/claude-logo-running.svg
                     - created: the plain logo, spinning
  webview/index.css  - point --monaco-monospace-font at the editor font, and
                       give each monospace rule whose own font-size is small
                       (under 14px, a fraction of an em, a percentage,
                       inherit, or the chat font size, possibly minus some
                       px) var(--vscode-editor-font-size) instead (tool
                       IN/OUT rows, markdown inline code outside replies,
                       slash-command echoes, permission requests, pills,
                       git summaries, changed-file lists, MCP dialogs,
                       ...);
                       also bring shrunken chat-font text back to the
                       normal chat size: the answered-AskUserQuestion
                       review box; the secondary line under tool headers
                       ("Answered · N questions", "Added N lines", ...);
                       and the "[Show Tab]" link in the tool headers of
                       Claude in Chrome;
                       set tool names in tool call headers (and the other
                       labels that share their class, such as "Advisor")
                       at weight 500 instead of bold, and the monospace
                       text after them (such as a file link) at the
                       x-height of the text around it; set off prompts in
                       the chat (in dark, non-high-contrast themes, with a
                       colored background
                       and near-white text); put Inter Display ahead of the
                       chat font (and give text inputs the page's font, and
                       the input footer's fixed-width texts tabular
                       digits); set the text of assistant replies at a line
                       height of 1.6 (in dark, non-high-contrast themes, in
                       #bbb, a little dimmer than Dark Modern's text), with
                       headings at weight 600 instead of bold, with 1em
                       between adjacent paragraphs that are not in a list
                       or a quote, and with inline code in the color
                       and at the x-height of the text around it, and with
                       no background or padding; draw markdown tables
                       with slightly brighter lines, rounded corners and
                       .5em of space at each side of a cell's text, and
                       in replies, 1em of space above and below each
                       table, like between paragraphs; draw VS Code's
                       square scrollbars instead of Chrome's, with vertical ones
                       14px wide (the editor's default), and keep the fade
                       at the bottom of the chat off its scrollbar; wrap
                       the command in a Bash (or PowerShell) tool call's IN
                       row; show a clipped tool call cell whose text is one
                       <pre> as exactly 3 lines, without the fade, and
                       under it, how many more lines it hides (counted by
                       an effect added in index.js); pulse the dot of the
                       input footer's Agents pill while it is the running
                       dot; fit the spinner row that the chat keeps for
                       background tasks (see index.js) on one line; draw
                       commit links (see index.js) without an underline,
                       like links that are children of paragraphs (unless
                       VS Code's accessibility.underlineLinks setting is
                       on)
  webview/index.js   - pass the family variable into the embedded Monaco
                       diff editors (they already take their size from the
                       size variable); hide the model picker's Default row,
                       checking the row the default resolves to instead;
                       show the tokens in context (e.g. "152k") right of the
                       input footer's prompt-cache clock, and keep that count
                       through API errors; stop pinning each prompt to the
                       top of the chat while its turn scrolls by, and show
                       it in full instead of behind "Show more"; make the
                       component that draws most tool calls' IN and OUT
                       cells (and the Write tool's preview) count the
                       lines that it hides; have the webview's own copy
                       listener make the copy of every selection (not just
                       of those with math in them), from when the webview
                       starts, and insert copy-as-markdown.js (from this
                       script's directory), which replaces the plain text
                       of such a copy with markdown when the selection
                       includes rendered markdown (copying commit links as
                       their text); insert commit-links.js, whose remark
                       plugin makes each commit ID in markdown with live
                       links a link to COMMIT_SCHEME + the ID, keep those
                       addresses through react-markdown's URL sanitizing,
                       and have a click on such a link ask the extension
                       to open its address, and its "Copy Link" copy the
                       ID; keep each session's background tasks that the
                       CLI counts as activity, and report a session as
                       running (for its tab icon) while any of them runs,
                       not just while an agent or a workflow does, and
                       show the input footer's Agents pill (which opens
                       the Agent map) also while any of those tasks that
                       are not agents runs, with their number in its
                       label; after a
                       turn, while any of those tasks runs, keep the
                       chat's spinner row, labeled with their number and
                       descriptions, opening the Agent map when clicked
  resources/native-binary/claude
                     - on NixOS only: point its ELF interpreter at the
                       system's dynamic loader (taken from
                       /run/current-system/sw/bin/sh), since the FHS loader
                       it expects (e.g. /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2) does
                       not exist there

There is no load-time integrity check on these files (VS Code verifies
extension signatures only at install time), so changing them where they are
installed is sufficient. The pristine originals are kept as .orig-font
backups and re-read as the input on every run, so re-running (even with
newly added patterns) is idempotent. Every file is written whole to a
.patching file and then renamed into place, so an interrupted run leaves no
half-written file (in particular, no truncated backup). Re-run after every
extension update, then reload the VS Code window; run with --undo to
restore the originals and delete the created files. The native binary gets
no backup (it is ~300 MB) and --undo leaves its interpreter alone, since
the binary cannot run on NixOS without it. Pass --no-mythos to leave Mythos
out of the model lineup, for accounts without access to it.

Usage: patch-claude-code [--undo] [--no-mythos]
"""

import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from collections.abc import Callable
from pathlib import Path

EXTENSIONS_ROOT = Path.home() / ".vscode-server" / "extensions"
EXTENSION_GLOB  = "anthropic.claude-code-*"
BACKUP_SUFFIX   = ".orig-font"
# The suffix of the file that new contents are written to before it is
# renamed over the file it replaces.
TEMP_SUFFIX     = ".patching"
NATIVE_BINARY   = "resources/native-binary/claude"
# Any system binary will do; its interpreter is the current glibc's loader.
NIXOS_LOADER_DONOR = "/run/current-system/sw/bin/sh"
# The file name of the tab icon this script adds to the extension's
# resources/, for a chat whose session is running.
RUNNING_ICON = "claude-logo-running.svg"
# The script that makes copying from the chat give markdown: a JS function
# expression, which index.js gets along with a call to it.
COPY_AS_MARKDOWN = Path(__file__).resolve().with_name("copy-as-markdown.js")
# The script that makes each commit ID in the chat a link that shows the
# commit: a JS function expression, which index.js and extension.js each
# get along with a call to it with COMMIT_SCHEME.
COMMIT_LINKS = Path(__file__).resolve().with_name("commit-links.js")
# The scheme of the addresses of the links that COMMIT_LINKS makes.
COMMIT_SCHEME = "git-commit:"

EDITOR_SIZE   = "var(--vscode-editor-font-size,12px)"
EDITOR_FAMILY = "var(--vscode-editor-font-family)"
# The font the chat's text is set in, ahead of the chat font: Inter Display,
# under both the name "InterDisplay" and its family name "Inter Display"
# (the only one of the two that Chrome on Linux matches). It must be
# installed where VS Code draws its window (with Remote SSH, the client);
# where it is not, the chat font is used.
TEXT_FONT = 'InterDisplay,"Inter Display"'
# A selector for assistant replies, and one (in an re.subn replacement
# string, with the markdown component's CSS-module hash as group 2) for the
# text in them.
REPLY      = "[data-testid=assistant-message]"
REPLY_TEXT = REPLY + r">.root_\2"
# CSS declarations that set an element's text (such as text in the editor
# font) at the x-height of its parent's font, whatever the fonts of the
# two: in a font-size, ex is the x-height of the parent's font, and
# font-size-adjust:.5 scales the element's font so that its x-height is
# half of the element's computed font-size. In a line of text, such an
# element can make the line taller than the others, unless it also gets a
# line height of 1.
PARENT_X_HEIGHT = "font-size:2ex;font-size-adjust:.5"
# The color of the lines of markdown tables: the input box's border color
# with 10% of the theme's text color mixed in, so a little more visible.
TABLE_LINE = "color-mix(in srgb,var(--app-input-border),var(--app-primary-foreground) 10%)"
# A CSS condition for browsers that draw scrollbars by ::-webkit-scrollbar
# rules, like Chrome (and so VS Code) does.
WEBKIT_SCROLLBARS = "@supports selector(::-webkit-scrollbar)"
# A JS function of a session's pccTasks (see index.js) that returns the
# label of the chat's spinner row while those tasks run, such as
# "2 background tasks: Review commit 6e3d7a7 · Watch CI" (naming the tasks
# that have a description), or false when there are none.
TASKS_LABEL = (
	'(pccTasks)=>pccTasks.length>0'
	'&&`${pccTasks.length} background ${pccTasks.length===1?"task":"tasks"}`'
	'+pccTasks.map((pccTask)=>pccTask.description).filter(Boolean)'
	'.map((description,index)=>(index>0?" · ":": ")+description).join("")'
)
# The editor's family variable, read at Monaco-editor construction time in JS.
MONACO_FONT_FAMILY = (
	'fontFamily:getComputedStyle(document.documentElement)'
	'.getPropertyValue("--vscode-editor-font-family").trim()||void 0'
)


# The chat font size variable, with or without a fallback value.
CHAT_SIZE = r"var\(--vscode-chat-font-size(?:,[^)]*)?\)"
# A font-size value that the webview uses for text no larger than its
# usual text: relative (em/%), sub-14px, inherit, or CHAT_SIZE, possibly
# minus some px. Larger sizes (14px and up for section titles and
# decorations, CHAT_SIZE plus some px) are deliberately left alone.
SMALL_SIZE = (
	r"(?:\.\d+em|(?:1[0-3]|[1-9])px|\d{1,2}(?:\.\d+)?%|inherit"
	r"|" + CHAT_SIZE + r"|calc\(" + CHAT_SIZE + r" - \d+px\))"
)
# A font-size declaration, not the end of a custom property's name such
# as --app-monospace-font-size.
FONT_SIZE = r"(?<![\w-])font-size:"
# The two custom properties the webview's monospace rules take their
# font-family from.
MONO_FAMILY = r"var\(--app-(?:monospace-font-family|font-family-mono)\)"

# The model picker's rows, in order, as (model ID, label).
MYTHOS = "claude-mythos-5-2"
MODEL_LINEUP = [
	(MYTHOS,             "Mythos 5.2"),
	("claude-fable-5-2", "Fable 5.2"),
	("claude-opus-5-5",  "Opus 5.5"),
	("claude-opus-4-8",  "Opus 4.8"),
	("claude-opus-4-6",  "Opus 4.6"),
]
# The model a session uses when no model is set, instead of the CLI's
# account-dependent default.
DEFAULT_MODEL = "claude-opus-5-5"
# {model ID: model ID} of lineup rows built when the CLI leaves them out,
# and the lineup row each copies its picker fields (supportedEffortLevels,
# supportsFastMode and the other supports* fields) from. For each pair,
# the CLI's model catalog gives both models the same values in those
# fields. The CLI hides Mythos whenever it lacks the server's model list.
STAND_INS = {MYTHOS: "claude-fable-5-2"}


def js_literal(value: object) -> str:
	"""Return `value` as a compact JSON (hence JS) literal that is also safe
	to use verbatim in an re.subn replacement string."""
	literal = json.dumps(value, separators=(",", ":"))
	assert "\\" not in literal, literal
	return literal


def cli_settings(lineup: list[tuple[str, str]]) -> str:
	"""Return, as a JS literal, the CLI settings that make DEFAULT_MODEL the
	default (an explicitly set model still wins) and whose modelPicker
	replaces the picker's built-in rows with `lineup`, (model ID, label)
	pairs in order. The CLI keeps its Default row in front of them, drops
	the rows the account can't use (and Mythos whenever it lacks the
	server's model list), appends the session's model when it is not among
	them, and describes each lineup row as "Custom model (<ID>)"."""
	options = [{"model": model, "label": label} for model, label in lineup]
	return js_literal({
		"env":         {"ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_MODEL": DEFAULT_MODEL},
		"modelPicker": {"replaceBuiltInOptions": True, "options": options},
	})


def models_fixup(lineup: list[tuple[str, str]]) -> str:
	"""Return a JS function expression mapping the CLI's initialize response
	to one whose models are the Default row, then a row per `lineup` entry
	((model ID, label) pairs) in order, then any other rows the CLI added.
	Lineup rows get their label and no description, so the picker shows
	just their names. A lineup row the CLI left out is built from its
	STAND_INS row, unless the CLI listed it as unavailable."""
	stand_ins = {model: STAND_INS[model] for model, _ in lineup if model in STAND_INS}
	# Besides the lineup rows, the CLI lists only the Default row and the
	# session's model, so a stand-in copied from any other row would vanish.
	assert set(stand_ins.values()) <= {model for model, _ in lineup}, stand_ins
	return (
		"(r)=>{let m=r?.models;if(!Array.isArray(m))return r;"
		f"let L={js_literal(lineup)},S={js_literal(stand_ins)},u=r.unavailable_models??[],"
		'o=m.filter((x)=>x.value!=="default"),f=(id,rows)=>rows.find((x)=>x.resolvedModel===id);'
		'return{...r,models:[...m.filter((x)=>x.value==="default"),...L.flatMap(([id,label])=>{'
		"let x=f(id,o);"
		"if(!x){let t=S[id]&&!f(id,u)&&f(S[id],o);if(!t)return[];x={...t,value:id,resolvedModel:id}}"
		'return[{...x,displayName:label,description:""}]}),'
		"...o.filter((x)=>!L.some(([id])=>id===x.resolvedModel))]}}"
	)


def non_agent_tasks(session: str) -> str:
	"""Return a JS expression for how many of the pccTasks (see index.js) of
	the session that the JS expression `session` gives are not agents."""
	return session + '.pccTasks.value.filter((pccTask)=>pccTask.type!=="local_agent").length'


def count_hidden_lines(cell: str) -> str:
	"""Return a JS effect callback for a tool call's clipped cell. While the
	cell hides N lines of its text, the callback keeps the data-pcc-more
	attribute of the cell's parent (a tool row, or for the Write tool, the
	tool body) set to "(N more lines)"; while the cell hides none, it keeps
	the attribute absent. `cell` is a JS expression for the cell element,
	evaluated when the effect runs. Only a cell whose text is a single <pre>
	(the cell's only child, or its only child's only child) is counted,
	with lines as tall as that <pre>'s line height. When cleaned
	up, the effect removes the attribute only if the cell has left the
	document; otherwise, the effect is about to run again for the cell's
	new children, and keeping the attribute stops the count from blinking."""
	return (
		f"()=>{{let pccCell={cell},pccRow=pccCell?.parentElement;if(!pccRow)return;"
		"let pccCount=()=>{"
		'let pccPre=pccCell.querySelector(":scope>pre:only-child,:scope>:only-child>pre:only-child"),'
		"pccLine=pccPre?parseFloat(getComputedStyle(pccPre).lineHeight):0,"
		"pccHidden=pccCell.scrollHeight-pccCell.clientHeight,"
		"pccLines=pccHidden>1&&pccLine>0?Math.ceil((pccHidden-1)/pccLine):0;"
		'if(pccLines>0)pccRow.dataset.pccMore=`(${pccLines} more line${pccLines===1?"":"s"})`;'
		"else delete pccRow.dataset.pccMore},"
		"pccObserver=new ResizeObserver(pccCount);"
		"pccObserver.observe(pccCell);"
		"for(let pccChild of pccCell.children)pccObserver.observe(pccChild);"
		"return()=>{pccObserver.disconnect();if(!pccCell.isConnected)delete pccRow.dataset.pccMore}}"
	)


# An re.subn replacement: a template string, or a function of the match.
Replacement = str | Callable[[re.Match[str]], str]
# A pattern: (compiled regex, replacement).
Pattern = tuple[re.Pattern[str], Replacement]
# Patterns that depend on each other, such as one that adds a variable and
# others that read it, so that a file must get either all of them or none
# (see apply_patterns).
Group = list[Pattern]


def patterns(mythos: bool) -> dict[str, list[Pattern | Group]]:
	"""Return {relative file path: [pattern or group of patterns]} for one
	extension directory. `mythos` says whether the model lineup includes
	Mythos."""
	lineup = [entry for entry in MODEL_LINEUP if mythos or entry[0] != MYTHOS]
	copy_as_markdown = COPY_AS_MARKDOWN.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
	commit_links     = COMMIT_LINKS.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
	scheme           = js_literal(COMMIT_SCHEME)
	return {
		"webview/index.css": [
			# Every rule that uses the chat font (the page's, which the rest
			# of the text inherits, and those of buttons, menus and headers)
			# puts TEXT_FONT ahead of it.
			(re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])font-family:var\(--vscode-chat-font-family\)"),
			 "font-family:" + TEXT_FONT + ",var(--vscode-chat-font-family)"),
			# Form controls don't inherit the page's font, and some of the
			# webview's (such as the session list's search box) set none, so
			# they were in Chrome's default control font. Make them inherit
			# it, after the page's rule; rules that set a control's font,
			# all more specific, still win.
			(re.compile(r"((?<=\})body\{[^}]*font-family:[^}]*\})"),
			 r"\1input,textarea,select{font-family:inherit}"),
			# The input footer's fixed-width texts (spans that it re-fits
			# its items around only when their length changes) get tabular
			# digits only inside its indicators, so with TEXT_FONT's
			# proportional digits, the tokens-in-context count (see
			# index.js), which is outside them, changed width without the
			# footer re-fitting. Give every fixed-width text tabular digits.
			(re.compile(r"(span\.indicator_[\w-]+ span\[data-footer-fixed-width\]\{[^}]*\})"),
			 r"\1[data-footer-fixed-width]{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}"),
			# Monaco widget font (hovers, suggest details).
			(re.compile(r"--monaco-monospace-font:"),
			 "--monaco-monospace-font:" + EDITOR_FAMILY + ","),
			# The "small monospace" size (branch pills, changed-file lists,
			# git summaries) becomes the plain editor size.
			(re.compile(r"--app-monospace-font-size-small:[^;}]+"),
			 "--app-monospace-font-size-small:" + EDITOR_SIZE),
			# Sweep: any rule that combines the monospace family with a
			# smaller font-size gets the editor size instead, in both
			# declaration orders (family first, then size first).
			(re.compile("(" + MONO_FAMILY + r"[^}]*?)" + FONT_SIZE + SMALL_SIZE + r"(?=[;}])"),
			 r"\1font-size:" + EDITOR_SIZE),
			(re.compile(FONT_SIZE + SMALL_SIZE + r"(;[^}]*?" + MONO_FAMILY + r")"),
			 "font-size:" + EDITOR_SIZE + r"\1"),
			# The read-only "Answered · N questions" review reuses the
			# question prompt's .95em/.9em/.85em text, which is sized for
			# the prompt's 1.1em container but renders tiny inside a 1em
			# tool body. Append a rule giving the review's text the normal
			# chat size; the CSS-module hash is captured from the reviewBody
			# rule, since it changes between versions.
			(re.compile(r"(\.reviewBody_([\w-]+)\{[^}]*\})"),
			 r"\1.reviewBody_\2 :is(.questionTextLarge_\2,.optionLabel_\2,"
			 r".optionDescription_\2){font-size:1em}"),
			# The secondary line under tool headers ("Answered · N
			# questions", "Added N lines", tool descriptions, "Edit failed",
			# ...) is shrunk to .85em; give it the normal chat size.
			(re.compile(r"(\.secondaryLine_[\w-]+\{[^}]*?)" + FONT_SIZE + r"[^;}]+"),
			 r"\1font-size:1em"),
			# The monospace text after the tool name in tool call headers
			# (a file path or a URL, often as a link, a search pattern, a
			# task ID, ...; the chat-font text there, such as Bash's
			# description, has another class) is at the editor size (made
			# so by the sweep above), so next to the tool name, it could
			# look smaller (as with a 15px chat font and a 12px editor
			# font). Set it at the x-height of the text around it. Its
			# class is also used in the Agent map's rows of tool calls and
			# for the "Open artifact ↗" link in some tool outputs. Its line
			# height becomes 1 only in a tool call's header: there, at the
			# chat's line height of 1.5, the larger text made a header
			# about 1-2px taller than one without it, but in the Agent
			# map's rows, it can be a flex item that hides what overflows
			# it, and a line height of 1 cut off the descenders of some
			# fonts (such as FreeMono).
			(re.compile(r"(\.toolNameTextSecondary_([\w-]+)\{[^}]*?)" + FONT_SIZE + r"[^;}]+([^}]*\})"),
			 r"\1" + PARENT_X_HEIGHT + r"\3.toolSummary_\2 .toolNameTextSecondary_\2{line-height:1}"),
			# The "[Show Tab]" link in the headers of Claude in Chrome's
			# tool calls is shrunk to .8em; give it the normal chat size.
			(re.compile(r"(\.tabLink_[\w-]+\{[^}]*?)" + FONT_SIZE + r"[^;}]+"),
			 r"\1font-size:1em"),
			# Tool names in tool call headers ("Bash", "Read", an MCP
			# tool's "Server [tool]"), and the few other labels that share
			# their class (such as "Advisor"), are bold; give them a
			# font-weight of 500, so they stand out less. TEXT_FONT draws
			# 500 in its Medium face. A font with no Medium face draws 500
			# in its Regular face, even if it has a SemiBold face (as
			# Segoe UI has).
			(re.compile(r"(\.toolNameText_[\w-]+\{[^}]*?(?<![\w-])font-weight:)[^;}]+"),
			 r"\g<1>500"),
			# Prompts in the chat share the input box's background; to make
			# them easier to spot among replies, give them a colored
			# background with near-white text in dark themes (VS Code marks
			# those with .vscode-dark; high-contrast ones get
			# .vscode-high-contrast instead), and elsewhere mix 10% of the
			# text color into their background.
			(re.compile(r"(\.userMessage_([\w-]+)\{[^}]*?background-color:)([^;}]+)([^}]*\})"),
			 r"\1color-mix(in srgb,\3,var(--app-primary-foreground) 10%)\4"
			 r".vscode-dark .userMessage_\2{background-color:#506195;color:hsla(0,0%,100%,.8)}"),
			# Restyle the text of assistant replies: the markdown component's
			# roots directly inside an assistant message (thinking, tool
			# output and dialogs render that component inside other
			# elements), found by the message's test ID, which every
			# supported version has (unlike its data-assistant-message
			# attribute, which is newer). The component's CSS-module hash is
			# captured from its inline-code rule. The text gets VS Code's
			# markdown preview's default line height of 1.6 (its code blocks
			# keep the chat's 1.5). Adjacent paragraphs directly in one root
			# (so not those in a list item or a quote) are 1em apart instead
			# of .2em: as far apart as the browser's default margins put a
			# top-level list from the paragraphs around it. Headings in the
			# text get a font-weight of 600 (as in the stylesheet of VS
			# Code's markdown preview) instead of the browser's 700, which
			# TEXT_FONT draws in its SemiBold face; a font with no face
			# between Regular and Bold draws 600 in its Bold face. Bold text
			# in a heading (such as all of `## **Title**`) gets the heading's
